#
# Known gaps in the release signing-order invariant, measured by
# scripts/release-signing-redprobe.sh. Override the path with
# $SIGNING_ORDER_EXCEPTIONS (which is how the probe proves this file is not a
# blanket: emptied, the probe goes red again).
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
# A finding the change in hand can close gets FIXED. A finding that needs work
# elsewhere gets NAMED here, together with the condition that deletes the entry.
# Neither gets switched off. The two obvious alternatives are both worse than
# the disease: a blocking gate left permanently red teaches every reader that
# "filestore checks is always red" is the normal state, and a real regression
# then rides through unnoticed; and marking the probe's job continue-on-error is
# the exact false-green pattern that probe was written to detect.
#
# FORMAT — one entry per line, everything after `#` is a comment:
#
#     <workflow path, relative to the repository root>::<job id>
#
# The key is FILE PLUS JOB, deliberately, not a file. A file-keyed entry would
# admit every present and future signing-order violation anywhere in that
# workflow — including one raised against a second publishing job somebody adds
# months from now — which is an allow-list, not an admission. This format admits
# exactly the one job it names.
#
# A line that is not exactly one path, one `::` and one job id is a HARD ERROR
# and the probe refuses to run at all. It is never a skipped line: a typo that
# quietly matched nothing would leave the gate red for a finding this file
# claims to have named, and the reader would have no way to see why. An entry
# naming a workflow that is not in the tree is refused for the same reason —
# it could never match, and so could never be reported stale either.
#
# A violation that is not attributable to a single job — a workflow with no tag
# trigger, a workflow in which no publishing step is recognised at all — has no
# job to name and therefore cannot be written here at all. Those must be fixed.
#
# THIS LIST SHRINKS, AND AN EMPTY FILE IS THE TARGET STATE. An entry whose
# file+job no longer violates is a HARD ERROR telling the reader to delete it.
# Without that the list would stop shrinking by itself and become a permanent
# allow-list wearing a temporary label: growth shows up in a diff a reviewer has
# to agree to, shrinkage does not, so the probe forces it. Every admitted gap
# also prints on every run, so a reader can tell "the gate found nothing" from
# "the gate found something we agreed to carry".
#
# The reason an entry is here, and the condition that deletes it, go in a
# comment IMMEDIATELY ABOVE that entry.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# .github/workflows/release.yml :: ghcr-image
#
# Reason: SIGN-AFTER-PUBLISH. `ghcr-image` runs docker/build-push-action with
# push: true and needs only [tag-format, security-gates, verify], while
# `sbom-and-sign` needs [..., ghcr-image, image-scan]. The pullable image
# reference therefore exists before any signature does. If sbom-and-sign fails —
# expired OIDC, Rekor unavailable, the SBOM action down — the image stays
# published under its release tag, pulls unsigned, and the red run retracts
# nothing. The probe is not misreporting: the ordering really is that way.
#
# Why not fixed here: closing it restructures the release pipeline — build
# without pushing, sign the digest, and apply the release tags only from a job
# downstream of signing — and which of those shapes to adopt is the owner's
# call on the release process, not a CI-wiring decision. The probe's own green
# control constructs one such shape, but that is a control proving the analyzer
# can return zero violations; it is not a proposed remedy.
#
# Removal condition: DELETE THIS ENTRY in the same commit that puts the
# publishing job downstream of `sbom-and-sign` (or otherwise stops it publishing
# before signing). The probe fails on a stale entry, so this is enforced rather
# than requested.
.github/workflows/release.yml::ghcr-image
